{"id":188606657,"slug":"tests-allies-and-enemies-answering","title":"Dan Karlin Went Back to Work In the Pandemic Era Bronx ICU","subtitle":"Dan Karlin Week, Part IV","post_date":"2026-02-22T12:25:18.339Z","audience":"everyone","type":"newsletter","canonical_url":"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/tests-allies-and-enemies-answering","wordcount":957,"source":"api","fetched_at":"2026-09-13T21:44:55.382Z","body_sha256":"2caf16cb2ea3d8079aec6d82ff3ba4a31a084c0b87665aa775243b2e5fd291fa","text_words":975,"complete":true,"body_html":"<p>Dan Karlin the the Chief Medical Officer at Definium.  However, when the pandemic hit, he was faculty at Albert Einstein.  The call went out to all medical professionals to serve in our hour of need. He answered the call.</p><p>Part I is <a href=\"https://open.substack.com/pub/thefrontierpsychiatrists/p/welcome-to-dan-karlin-md-week?r=1ct8f&amp;utm_campaign=post&amp;utm_medium=web\">here.</a></p><p>Part II is<a href=\"https://open.substack.com/pub/thefrontierpsychiatrists/p/what-is-a-psychedelic?r=1ct8f&amp;utm_campaign=post&amp;utm_medium=web\"> here.</a></p><p>Part III is <a href=\"https://open.substack.com/pub/thefrontierpsychiatrists/p/language-the-self-and-psychotherapy?r=1ct8f&amp;utm_campaign=post&amp;utm_medium=web\">here</a>.</p><p>Welcome to <em>Dan Karlin Week, Part IV</em></p><div><hr></div><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg\" width=\"572\" height=\"429\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:768,&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:572,&quot;bytes&quot;:540413,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/i/188606657?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!bYrs!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8ff34de8-faaa-432d-8b56-6e938b8fe7ab_1024x768.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class=\"image-caption\">(C) Owen Muir, 2025</figcaption></figure></div><p><strong>OM:</strong> So you’re a measurement guy. You clearly have a real background in psychotherapy, psychodynamics, the traditions that make psychiatry a unique subspecialty in medicine. And your friend calls you up in the middle of COVID — and you’d spent some time using your skills as a physician. You can tell me if this is okay to say, but you went to the ICU and did some work as a regular old doctor, keeping people alive in New York City, which at the time was ground zero for the pandemic.</p><p><strong>DK:</strong> God, I don’t remember exactly what dates, but it must have been April. It was in the Bronx. I had been associated with the Montefiore system for a little teaching — I was credentialed, I covered a service every once in a while or an inpatient service if someone called out last minute, because I lived in the neighborhood and was running the digital health company. So if they needed me, I was happy to duck in and do what I could to help. Plus, I love the work, and the thing that working in industry takes away is the opportunity to see patients as much. So I really appreciated that department and the people who worked in it.</p><p>When things were clearly taking a turn — there was a day that New York sort of went from playfully arm-bumping instead of handshaking to a degree of panic — a general call was put out, including in the Department of Psychiatry: did anyone want to be helpful in the event they were needed? My background: I grew up on ambulances from when I was sixteen. When I was at Columbia, I ran their ambulance service on the Upper West Side of Manhattan, where I’m originally from. When 9/11 happened, because of my role there, even though I wasn’t in the city at the time — I was in Western Massachusetts — I was able to get to the city pretty quickly, and spent three days at the World Trade Center scene helping out.</p><p>With this pretty clearly impending disaster, particularly up in the Bronx, it was obvious that participating to the extent I was able would be helpful. My EMS training and longstanding interest in disaster medicine and overwhelmed systems made me think I could be useful. So I got a call one Saturday that a team was running out of docs, and I went into a hospital up there and found a terribly overwhelmed system. The team I joined was taking care of twelve patients, four of whom were ventilated, several more on aggressive respiratory interventions but not yet intubated. There was one pulse ox for the four ventilated patients. If you wanted a blood gas — which, for folks not in medicine, is how you monitor how well you’re doing breathing for someone — you could draw one and walk it down to the lab yourself. That was the level of being overwhelmed.</p><p>On the second day there, we ended up doing fourteen resuscitations over twelve hours — unexpected deaths. People who were sort of expected to die were intubated because that decision had been made, but these were people dying unexpectedly. It was as badly overwhelmed as I’ve seen any system.</p><p><strong>OM:</strong> Did any of them survive those resuscitations?</p><p><strong>DK:</strong> Not to leave the hospital. You get a pulse back and keep moving. But it’s not like on TV. CPR doesn’t always work — in fact, most of the time it doesn’t. Especially when you’re dealing with an overwhelmed system. And so early in the experience of the pandemic, in many cases we weren’t even sure why we were seeing what we were seeing. There was the respiratory pathology, obviously, but there was also a lot of clotting going on that we just didn’t know was a feature of serious COVID — people getting pulmonary embolisms even after they seemed in the clear. And again, it was early. Everyone was doing their absolute best.</p><p>I spent two days there and left glad that I was able to help, but completely overwhelmed on behalf of the residents and staff physicians who had to keep going back. I got to leave because someone else got better enough to come in. I was glad I could do what little I could and, in many cases, just witness the heroic work that the folks there were doing.</p><p>One thing I could do — and I felt fortunate to be able to do it — was make calls to families whose loved ones were in these units and dying, and try to talk through that with them in a way that an incredibly busy internal medicine resident neither had the time nor the training to do. Trying to bring, I think like so much of what we do, some relief to a situation that is intrinsically difficult if not impossible. And again, this comes right back to where we were: trying to help people form a story around their experience that will allow them to move forward — with as much self-realization, self-actualization, sense of efficacy, sense of purpose as possible. So much of even that situation, once you’re done dealing with the acute physiological needs of a person, is about trying to help them tell themselves a story about the situation they’re in.</p><div><hr></div><p>Dan Week Part V is coming next!</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe now&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:null}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/tests-allies-and-enemies-answering?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email&utm_content=share&action=share&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Share&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:null}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/tests-allies-and-enemies-answering?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email&utm_content=share&action=share\"><span>Share</span></a></p><p>Prior storytelling about the pandemic era is available below:</p><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;cb790d8c-2465-4a81-b8b6-3e74898c24e6&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;In the pandemic, I didn’t know what to do with myself. One of the things I did to make sense of my world falling apart was to make a podcast. I think the questions I asked at the time hold up well enough. This is the first episode I made, just about a week into lockdown in New York City. I recorded the audio on March 13th and released it on March 15…&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;I Explained The Pandemic to Myself: Episode 1&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100},{&quot;id&quot;:5794437,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Michelle Bernabe, RN&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;rethinking health across body, community, and sy