m paid for.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://bucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/62e4f131-0d58-4d2b-965c-4f226291bcd6_720x720.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:true,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:null,&quot;primaryPublicationSubscribeUrl&quot;:&quot;https://interventionalpsychiatrist.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationUrl&quot;:&quot;https://interventionalpsychiatrist.substack.com&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationName&quot;:&quot;Carlene MacMillan, MD&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationId&quot;:2879781}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2023-11-14T12:20:22.115Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1eam!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F6c88b137-0364-4456-8235-4177b74e90c0_3088x2316.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/i-explained-the-pandemic-to-myself-295&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:138802738,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:3,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;4f5328ad-feb9-4be9-86af-1b6b43a40a7d&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists is a health-themed newsletter and podcast. It’s a little bit of a media empire; there is a YouTube channel, Instagram, TikTok, and music I do over on Spotify up top. This article is a bit of a meta-article on my journey, with historical audio as reference material.&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;I Explained the Pandemic To Myself: Episode 3&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100},{&quot;id&quot;:5794437,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Michelle Bernabe, RN&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;rethinking health across body, community, and system. unlearning, healing, moral imagination, and regenerative change.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/27dec043-bed9-44ba-a3fb-2eb2de463f6e_1600x1600.png&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:true,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:null,&quot;primaryPublicationSubscribeUrl&quot;:&quot;https://moralhealth.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationUrl&quot;:&quot;https://moralhealth.substack.com&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationName&quot;:&quot;Moral Health&quot;,&quot;primaryPublicationId&quot;:77106}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2023-11-15T14:13:43.067Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!MHd1!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fedd673fe-1f46-47ac-976c-d43dd9f4222b_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/i-explained-the-pandemic-to-myself-db8&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:138820395,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:3,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;57dda6c8-9989-45c0-9862-d59fbcf8bee9&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;I’m Owen. This week of posts is different from the usual…it’s a bit of an origin story for this newsletter, The Frontier Psychiatrists. It’s a daily health-themed newsletter by Imperfectionist physician Owen Scott Muir, M.D. There is a podcast (this),&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;I Explained The Pandemic To Myself: Episode 4&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2023-11-16T14:14:39.159Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BBpF!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F64395b67-d38a-4ee0-8d47-fc2b412309ab_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/i-explained-the-pandemic-to-myself-f53&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:138821015,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:2,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;ec687912-d429-4fcf-b9e4-81c71c0b3c86&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists is a daily health-themed publication. Your author’s love of podcasting is well established, and today, another from the vaults, dating back to the pandemic. In this episode, which I called “The Wave,” I started getting a little more ambitious in production, and strange in the jagged nature of my storytelling. I interview Jo…&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;I Explained The Pandemic to Myself: Episode 5&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2024-03-15T18:37:05.102Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!TdyP!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4fce81d0-c434-4174-8c51-a303cf1adc3e_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/i-explained-the-pandemic-to-myself-dcd&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:138942693,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:4,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div><div class=\"digest-post-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;nodeId&quot;:&quot;20d9d195-4a78-4d0c-b192-eaaa03dd28ad&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;Most of this was recorded over a year or more ago. It’s strange to hear it now, but I also captured how much this experience made us all feel a lot of feelings. It’s worth hearing, if in a different context than we might have at the time.&quot;,&quot;cta&quot;:&quot;Listen now&quot;,&quot;showBylines&quot;:true,&quot;showDescription&quot;:true,&quot;showImage&quot;:true,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;md&quot;,&quot;isEditorNode&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The End of The Pandemic Podcast&quot;,&quot;publishedBylines&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:2277375,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Owen Scott Muir, M.D, DFAACAP&quot;,&quot;bio&quot;:&quot;Dr. Owen Scott Muir, M.D., DFAACAP is the Chief Medical Officer of Radial &amp; Neurolief. He's a graduate of Amherst College, Child, Adolescent, and Adult Psychiatrist, and a prolific writer and podcaster. https://www.meetradial.com for more.&quot;,&quot;photo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1c03f44d-4506-4843-989f-0d38e66c6946_400x400.jpeg&quot;,&quot;is_guest&quot;:false,&quot;bestseller_tier&quot;:100}],&quot;post_date&quot;:&quot;2023-04-16T23:48:12.888Z&quot;,&quot;cover_image&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!T2sw!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1b817deb-e407-411b-a78c-6f5547238671_1844x1436.png&quot;,&quot;cover_image_alt&quot;:null,&quot;canonical_url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/p/the-end-of-the-pandemic-podcast&quot;,&quot;section_name&quot;:null,&quot;video_upload_id&quot;:null,&quot;id&quot;:115271135,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;podcast&quot;,&quot;reaction_count&quot;:4,&quot;comment_count&quot;:0,&quot;publication_id&quot;:355550,&quot;publication_name&quot;:&quot;The Frontier Psychiatrists &quot;,&quot;publication_logo_url&quot;:&quot;https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oO3w!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fbucketeer-e05bbc84-baa3-437e-9518-adb32be77984.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F997b8496-9b4d-43c2-b393-6f8a77945b00_1080x1080.png&quot;,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;youtube_url&quot;:null,&quot;show_links&quot;:null,&quot;feed_url&quot;:null}\"></div>","text":"Dan Karlin the the Chief Medical Officer at Definium. However, when the pandemic hit, he was faculty at Albert Einstein. The call went out to all medical professionals to serve in our hour of need. He answered the call.\n\nPart I is here.\n\nPart II is here.\n\nPart III is here .\n\nWelcome to Dan Karlin Week, Part IV\n\n(C) Owen Muir, 2025\n\nOM: So you’re a measurement guy. You clearly have a real background in psychotherapy, psychodynamics, the traditions that make psychiatry a unique subspecialty in medicine. And your friend calls you up in the middle of COVID — and you’d spent some time using your skills as a physician. You can tell me if this is okay to say, but you went to the ICU and did some work as a regular old doctor, keeping people alive in New York City, which at the time was ground zero for the pandemic.\n\nDK: God, I don’t remember exactly what dates, but it must have been April. It was in the Bronx. I had been associated with the Montefiore system for a little teaching — I was credentialed, I covered a service every once in a while or an inpatient service if someone called out last minute, because I lived in the neighborhood and was running the digital health company. So if they needed me, I was happy to duck in and do what I could to help. Plus, I love the work, and the thing that working in industry takes away is the opportunity to see patients as much. So I really appreciated that department and the people who worked in it.\n\nWhen things were clearly taking a turn — there was a day that New York sort of went from playfully arm-bumping instead of handshaking to a degree of panic — a general call was put out, including in the Department of Psychiatry: did anyone want to be helpful in the event they were needed? My background: I grew up on ambulances from when I was sixteen. When I was at Columbia, I ran their ambulance service on the Upper West Side of Manhattan, where I’m originally from. When 9/11 happened, because of my role there, even though I wasn’t in the city at the time — I was in Western Massachusetts — I was able to get to the city pretty quickly, and spent three days at the World Trade Center scene helping out.\n\nWith this pretty clearly impending disaster, particularly up in the Bronx, it was obvious that participating to the extent I was able would be helpful. My EMS training and longstanding interest in disaster medicine and overwhelmed systems made me think I could be useful. So I got a call one Saturday that a team was running out of docs, and I went into a hospital up there and found a terribly overwhelmed system. The team I joined was taking care of twelve patients, four of whom were ventilated, several more on aggressive respiratory interventions but not yet intubated. There was one pulse ox for the four ventilated patients. If you wanted a blood gas — which, for folks not in medicine, is how you monitor how well you’re doing breathing for someone — you could draw one and walk it down to the lab yourself. That was the level of being overwhelmed.\n\nOn the second day there, we ended up doing fourteen resuscitations over twelve hours — unexpected deaths. People who were sort of expected to die were intubated because that decision had been made, but these were people dying unexpectedly. It was as badly overwhelmed as I’ve seen any system.\n\nOM: Did any of them survive those resuscitations?\n\nDK: Not to leave the hospital. You get a pulse back and keep moving. But it’s not like on TV. CPR doesn’t always work — in fact, most of the time it doesn’t. Especially when you’re dealing with an overwhelmed system. And so early in the experience of the pandemic, in many cases we weren’t even sure why we were seeing what we were seeing. There was the respiratory pathology, obviously, but there was also a lot of clotting going on that we just didn’t know was a feature of serious COVID — people getting pulmonary embolisms even after they seemed in the clear. And again, it was early. Everyone was doing their absolute best.\n\nI spent two days there and left glad that I was able to help, but completely overwhelmed on behalf of the residents and staff physicians who had to keep going back. I got to leave because someone else got better enough to come in. I was glad I could do what little I could and, in many cases, just witness the heroic work that the folks there were doing.\n\nOne thing I could do — and I felt fortunate to be able to do it — was make calls to families whose loved ones were in these units and dying, and try to talk through that with them in a way that an incredibly busy internal medicine resident neither had the time nor the training to do. Trying to bring, I think like so much of what we do, some relief to a situation that is intrinsically difficult if not impossible. And again, this comes right back to where we were: trying to help people form a story around their experience that will allow them to move forward — with as much self-realization, self-actualization, sense of efficacy, sense of purpose as possible. So much of even that situation, once you’re done dealing with the acute physiological needs of a person, is about trying to help them tell themselves a story about the situation they’re in.\n\nDan Week Part V is coming next!\n\nSubscribe now\n\nShare\n\nPrior storytelling about the pandemic era is available below:"}